HYPOTHESIS (final)
==================
In the Barcelona province, the gardening introduction pathway shows two contrasting
"prevalence styles" that are conditioned by the same habitat-context gradient
(lowland human footprint vs. upland naturalness), but expressed on different axes:

(1) FREQUENCY STYLE — pervasive but concentrated in lowland, warm, human-modified
    habitats. Gardening-introduced species are present in the highest fraction of
    plots in Urban (48%), Wetlands (50%), Coastal (45%), Riparian (36%) and Cropland
    (21%) habitats, and are absent/near-absent in high-elevation natural habitats
    (Meadows 8%, Coniferous forests 6.5%, Broad-leaved forests 4.4%, Scrublands
    1.9%, Rock outcrops 0%). Occurrence is conditioned by the lowland climatic and
    road/urban-proximity context: 38% of plots below 200 m hold gardening species,
    falling to 0% above 900 m; in a joint logistic model only elevation (OR 0.29 per
    1 SD; univariate OR 0.23) and distance to road (OR 0.72) remain significant, and
    raw urbanization is individually a strong positive predictor (OR 1.85 per SD)
    that is absorbed by elevation because urban plots sit on low-elevation ground.
    Fisher exact: gardening occurrence odds are ~20x higher in lowland-humanized
    (Urban/Wetlands/Coastal/Riparian) than in upland-natural (forest/scrub/rock/
    meadow) habitats (p < 1e-30).

(2) DOMINANCE STYLE — minor where invasion is intense, exclusive where it is rare.
    Conditional on a plot being invaded, gardening is a demographic MINORITY of the
    local introduced flora in the most humanized, intensively used contexts
    (mean gardening share of invaded flora: Croplands 0.17, Urban 0.29) where
    unintentional introductions dominate, but it becomes the MAJORITY or sole
    introduced flora in less humanized natural and semi-natural contexts (share:
    Wetlands 0.65, Coastal 0.53, Riparian 0.44, Coniferous forests 0.90, Broad-
    leaved forests 1.00). A weighted binomial GLM over invaded plots shows per-plot
    gardening share decreases with surrounding urban cover (OR 0.73 per 1 SD,
    p<0.001) and cropland cover (OR 0.67, p<0.001) and increases with distance from
    roads (OR 1.22, p<0.001); tercile contrasts corroborate the agricultural effect
    (share 0.23 in highest-cropland tercile vs 0.37 baseline, p=0.024; urban terciles
    decline 0.41->0.31).

(3) COMPOSITION + TEMPORAL BACKING — the gardening-imported species are predominantly
    woody/perennial horticultural taxa (20 shrubs/trees + 18 perennial forbs of 42
    species), tall (mean 6.5 m), with narrow invaded-niche breadth (0.45) and short
    mean residence times (86 y), consistent with ornamental escapes planted near
    human settlements and their subsequent spread; and the gardening pathway is the
    dominant introduction route only in the most recent period (75% of 1985-2019
    introductions), i.e. the "frequent+minority" style in lowland urban contexts is a
    recent phenomenon tied to ornamental planting, whereas its rare-but-exclusive
    presence in upland forests/scrub reflects isolated horticultural escapes at the
    edges of humanized lowlands.

SYNTHESIS: The gardening pathway is frequent, recent and numerically secondary in
lowland, warm, urbanized, road-proximal habitats, but rare and demographically
dominant (often the only introduced species) in high-elevation, cool, natural,
isolated habitat types — i.e., its prevalence styles shift from "pervasive but
outnumbered by unintentional introductions" in lowland human-impacted contexts to
"sparse but exclusive" in upland natural contexts.
